{"timestamp": "2026-01-16 02:59:30", "level": "DEBUG", "module": "anthropic._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/v1/messages', 'timeout': Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600), 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-5cd56da3-c40a-41ec-bba1-41ed57c4a724', 'json_data': {'max_tokens': 4096, 'messages': [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Please complete the following task:\n\n{\n  "requirements_file": "src/field/ensemble_ui/output/agent-cost-tracking-enhancement/requirements.md",\n  "project_name": "Agent Cost Tracking Enhancement",\n  "output_file": "/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md"\n}\n\nRemember to respond with valid JSON matching the expected output format.\n'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01N36tLQZ8LeEYsXTVACoMEK', 'name': 'read_file', 'input': {'file_path': 'src/field/ensemble_ui/output/agent-cost-tracking-enhancement/requirements.md'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01N36tLQZ8LeEYsXTVACoMEK', 'content': '{"success": true, "content": "# Requirements: Agent Cost Tracking Enhancement\\n\\n## Vision\\nEnhance the ensemble UI to display cost estimates, execution duration, and model information for each agent task. This will provide better visibility into agent performance and resource usage.\\n\\n## Objectives\\n1. Display execution cost estimates for each agent in the agent summary\\n2. Show start time, completion time, and duration of execution\\n3. Tag each agent with the model it used during execution\\n4. Ensure data flows from backend tracking to frontend display\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n1. **Backend Enhancements**:\\n   - Capture start time, end time, and duration for agent executions\\n   - Track the model used by each agent (from AgentRuntime)\\n   - Calculate cost estimates based on token usage and model pricing\\n   - Include this data in activity tracking and agent states\\n\\n2. **Frontend Enhancements**:\\n   - Display cost estimate in AgentSummaryPane component\\n   - Show execution duration (started \\u2192 completed)\\n   - Display model tag/badge for each agent\\n   - Format costs in a user-friendly way (e.g., \\"$0.0042\\")\\n   - Format durations in appropriate units (ms, seconds, minutes)\\n\\n3. **Data Flow**:\\n   - Extend Activity Tracker to capture cost and model data\\n   - Update API endpoints to return this information\\n   - Update AgentSummaryPane to consume and display the data\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n- Historical cost aggregation/reporting (future feature)\\n- Budget alerts or limits (future feature)\\n- Per-tool cost breakdown (future feature)\\n- Real-time cost streaming during execution (will show on completion)\\n\\n## Technical Constraints\\n- Must work with existing activity_tracker.py system\\n- Should integrate with existing AgentRuntime metrics\\n- Frontend changes limited to AgentSummaryPane component\\n- Backend must be backward compatible with existing agent executions\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n1. \\u2705 Each completed agent shows:\\n   - Start time (ISO format or relative time)\\n   - Completion time (ISO format or relative time)\\n   - Duration (e.g., \\"2.3s\\", \\"1m 45s\\")\\n   - Cost estimate (e.g., \\"$0.0042\\")\\n   - Model tag (e.g., \\"claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\\")\\n\\n2. \\u2705 Data is captured reliably from backend\\n3. \\u2705 UI displays information in a readable, compact format\\n4. \\u2705 No performance degradation to existing functionality\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n1. AgentRuntime already tracks token usage and model selection\\n2. Model pricing information is available (from model_selector.py or similar)\\n3. Activity tracker can be extended without breaking existing consumers\\n4. Frontend has access to activity data via WebSocket or polling\\n\\n## User Stories\\n1. **As a developer**, I want to see how much each agent execution costs so I can optimize expensive operations\\n2. **As a developer**, I want to see execution duration so I can identify slow agents\\n3. **As a developer**, I want to see which model was used so I can correlate performance with model choice\\n\\n## Technical Approach\\n\\n### Backend Changes\\n1. Extend `record_agent_completed()` in activity_tracker.py to accept:\\n   - `started_at`: ISO timestamp\\n   - `completed_at`: ISO timestamp  \\n   - `duration_ms`: milliseconds\\n   - `model_used`: model identifier\\n   - `cost_estimate`: float (USD)\\n\\n2. Update AgentRuntime to:\\n   - Track start time on initialization\\n   - Calculate cost based on token usage and model pricing\\n   - Pass this data to activity tracker on completion\\n\\n3. Update API response in main.py to include these fields in agent state\\n\\n### Frontend Changes\\n1. Update AgentSummaryPane.jsx to:\\n   - Display execution timeline (started \\u2192 completed)\\n   - Show duration badge\\n   - Show cost estimate badge\\n   - Show model tag\\n   - Handle formatting for readability\\n\\n### Data Structure\\n```javascript\\n// Expected agent info structure\\n{\\n  agent_id: \\"exec_dir_1\\",\\n  type: \\"executive_director\\",\\n  status: \\"completed\\",\\n  started_at: \\"2024-01-13T10:30:00Z\\",\\n  completed_at: \\"2024-01-13T10:30:42Z\\",\\n  duration_ms: 42000,\\n  model_used: \\"claude-3-5-sonnet-20241022\\",\\n  cost_estimate: 0.0042,\\n  // ... existing fields\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## Implementation Notes\\n- Cost calculation should use actual token counts from Claude API responses\\n- If token counts unavailable, estimate based on character counts with clear \\"~\\" prefix\\n- Duration should handle both running (show elapsed) and completed states\\n- Model tags should be shortened for display (e.g., \\"sonnet-3.5\\" instead of full name)\\n"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_01UdLKRXN7SjHu8UpRFMKyHH', 'name': 'write_file', 'input': {'file_path': '/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md', 'content': '# Architecture Proposal: Agent Cost Tracking Enhancement\n\n## 1. Architecture Overview\n### Design Pattern: Event-Driven Microservice Extension\nWe\'ll extend the existing system with minimal invasive changes, using an event-driven approach to capture and display agent execution metrics.\n\n**Key Design Principles:**\n- Backward compatibility\n- Minimal performance overhead\n- Clean separation of concerns\n- Extensible metric tracking\n\n## 2. Tech Stack\n### Backend\n- **Language**: Python (existing system)\n- **Frameworks**: \n  - Existing activity_tracker.py \n  - AgentRuntime framework\n- **Libraries**:\n  - `datetime` for timestamp handling\n  - `json` for data serialization\n  - Existing cost calculation libraries\n\n### Frontend\n- **Framework**: React (existing AgentSummaryPane)\n- **State Management**: Existing state management \n- **Formatting Libraries**: \n  - `date-fns` for time formatting\n  - `numeral.js` for currency formatting\n\n## 3. System Components\n\n### Backend Components\n1. **Activity Tracker Extension**\n   - Responsibility: Capture and store agent execution metrics\n   - Input: Agent execution details\n   - Output: Enhanced agent state with cost and timing information\n\n2. **AgentRuntime Modifier**\n   - Responsibility: Calculate execution metrics during agent runs\n   - Tracks start time, token usage, model selection\n   - Computes cost estimates\n\n3. **API Endpoint Updater**\n   - Responsibility: Include new metric fields in agent state responses\n   - Ensures backward compatibility\n   - Provides standardized metric format\n\n### Frontend Components\n1. **AgentSummaryPane Renderer**\n   - Responsibility: Display agent execution metrics\n   - Render cost, duration, model information\n   - Handle formatting and readability\n\n## 4. Data Flow Diagram\n```\nAgentRuntime \n  ↓ (execution metrics)\nActivity Tracker \n  ↓ (store & compute)\nAPI Endpoint \n  ↓ (transmit)\nAgentSummaryPane\n  ↓ (render)\nUser Interface\n```\n\n## 5. File/Directory Structure\n```\nsrc/\n├── backend/\n│   ├── activity_tracker.py        # Extended tracking\n│   ├── agent_runtime.py           # Metric calculation\n│   └── api/\n│       └── main.py                # Updated endpoints\n└── frontend/\n    └── components/\n        └── AgentSummaryPane.jsx   # Updated renderer\n```\n\n## 6. Data Model Extensions\n```python\nclass AgentExecutionMetrics:\n    started_at: datetime\n    completed_at: datetime\n    duration_ms: int\n    model_used: str\n    cost_estimate: float\n    token_usage: int\n```\n\n## 7. API Design\n### Endpoint: `/api/agent_state`\n**Response Extension:**\n```json\n{\n  "agent_id": "string",\n  "status": "string",\n  "metrics": {\n    "started_at": "ISO8601 timestamp",\n    "completed_at": "ISO8601 timestamp",\n    "duration_ms": 42000,\n    "model_used": "claude-3-5-sonnet",\n    "cost_estimate": 0.0042\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## 8. Deployment Strategy\n- No additional infrastructure required\n- Can be deployed as part of regular frontend/backend update\n- Feature flag recommended for gradual rollout\n\n## 9. Testing Strategy\n### Backend Tests\n- Unit tests for metric calculation\n- Integration tests for API compatibility\n- Verify cost estimation accuracy\n\n### Frontend Tests\n- Component rendering tests\n- Snapshot testing for AgentSummaryPane\n- Formatting and display tests\n\n## 10. Risks and Mitigations\n1. **Performance Overhead**\n   - Mitigation: Lightweight metric tracking\n   - Minimal additional computation\n\n2. **Incomplete Metric Tracking**\n   - Mitigation: Graceful fallback\n   - Display partial information if complete data unavailable\n\n## 11. Alternatives Considered\n1. **Full Rewrite of Tracking System**\n   - Rejected: Too invasive\n   - Current approach more maintainable\n\n2. **External Metrics Service**\n   - Rejected: Adds unnecessary complexity\n   - Current approach more integrated\n\n## 12. Open Questions\n- Precise token pricing for different models\n- Exact method of cost estimation for edge cases\n\n## 13. Implementation Notes\n- Use existing infrastructure\n- Minimal changes to current system\n- Easily extensible for future enhancements'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_01UdLKRXN7SjHu8UpRFMKyHH', 'content': '{"success": true, "message": "File written successfully to /Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture.md (backup: /Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260116_025930.md)", "backup_path": "/Users/mattbillock/.ensemble/backups/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/architecture_20260116_025930.md", "was_overwrite": true}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 97.0%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.5\n- Common errors to avoid: CircuitBreakerOpenError, RateLimitError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Watch out for these common errors: CircuitBreakerOpenError, RateLimitError\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The architecture proposal successfully addresses the requirements by providing a lightweight, flexible approach to adding new achievements. By focusing on modularity and compatibility, the design mini...\n- The architecture proposal successfully addresses the visibility requirements by proposing targeted, minimally invasive changes to the frontend and backend. The design maintains the existing system\'s c...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current